  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/macrobrachium-rosenbergii-3/",
      "html": "<p>Macrobrachium rosenbergii is the largest species of freshwater shrimp. Males can reach a length of 50 cm (including the long arms), as shown by specimens photographed on a food market in Thailand. Despite this enormous size, the species does not live long: scientific research found that the shrimps live only 1.5 years.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/macrobrachium-rosenbergii-3/",
      "html": "<p>In their lifetime the shrimps sometimes wander more than 1,000 km upstream in rivers and back again, because the larvae can hatch only in a marine environment.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/macrobrachium-rosenbergii-3/",
      "html": "<p>Macrobrachium rosenbergii is heavily used in aquaculture in southeast Asia. Medium-sized specimens (body length without the arms 6–8 cm) are imported regularly. Some females of that size have spawned.</p>"
    }
  ],
